\begin{lemma}[Obstruction at zero scalar ratio]
\label{lem:ancient-zero-ratio-obstruction}
There is no slice $t_0\leq0$ and center $p$ for which, for every
$\varepsilon>0$, the inequality
$R(t_0,x)d_{t_0}(p,x)^2\leq\varepsilon$ holds outside a
sufficiently large ball.
\lean{PoincareMT.RicciFlow.curvature_eq_zero_of_zero_ratio}
\uses{prop:rf-bounded-harnack}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Rescale the quadratic-decay annuli and retain overlapping flat chart limits.
Their radial coordinates and metric jets construct a connected smooth link and
a flat cone. Backward control supplies the Euclidean umbilic realization of
that same link. Its chordal metric gives the Euclidean cone-volume lower
bound. Bishop--Gromov rigidity forces the original slice to be flat,
contradicting nonflatness and bounded ancient Harnack.
\end{proof}

